# serial_cycle_wide -- a ring of four, and the first ring with room in it.
#
# serial_cycle closed a lap over three domains and proved a ring has no
# vantage point. It also, quietly, had no room: in a ring of three every
# pair of domains is adjacent, so any two of them share a region and no two
# may ever run at the same moment. A ring of three is entirely serial, and
# nothing about that is a policy -- it is what the grants add up to.
#
# This declaration adds one domain and keeps every other property. `alder`
# writes `ask_ab` and reads `ask_da`; `birch` writes `ask_bc` and reads
# `ask_ab`; `cedar` writes `ask_cd` and reads `ask_bc`; `dogwood` writes
# `ask_da` and reads `ask_cd`. Every region keeps exactly one writer and one
# reader, every domain spans exactly two peers, and exactly one directed
# walk visits all four domains once and comes home.
#
# What the fourth domain buys is the pairs that are not adjacent. `alder`
# and `cedar` sit opposite one another and hold no region in common;
# `birch` and `dogwood` do the same. Those two pairs are the whole
# concurrency this shape permits, and a ring of n domains permits
# n * (n - 3) / 2 of them -- zero at three, two at four.
#
# `taken` is alder's own tail, the one number about a completed lap that
# alder alone may write, read by dogwood, its predecessor. It joins a pair
# that is already adjacent, so it takes no concurrency away.
#
# Read whole by `caravan/read.rye`; exercised by `caravan/concurrent.rye`.

system serial_cycle_wide

domain alder
domain birch
domain cedar
domain dogwood

channel alder birch
channel birch cedar
channel cedar dogwood
channel dogwood alder

region ask_ab 4096
region ask_bc 4096
region ask_cd 4096
region ask_da 4096
region taken 64

grant ask_ab alder rw
grant ask_ab birch r
grant ask_bc birch rw
grant ask_bc cedar r
grant ask_cd cedar rw
grant ask_cd dogwood r
grant ask_da dogwood rw
grant ask_da alder r
grant taken alder rw
grant taken dogwood r
